-- title:
--   Image of the zero component equals its generic point's closure
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a Deligne–Rapoport model package $\mathfrak{X}$ for $p$, i.e. an element of `DRModelPackage p` recording the integral two-chart model `DRModel p` over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$ together with its properness, flatness, integrality and normality data, its rational and geometric generic curve models with their Galois compatibilities, the sections `εinf`, `εzero`, the smooth locus, and the further data of the structure, among them the curve models `𝔛.ratModel κ` and the component morphisms `𝔛.compZero`. Let $O$ be a local commutative ring whose maximal ideal is the principal ideal generated by the image of $p$, let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and let $\mathrm{to}\kappa\colon O \to \kappa$ be a ring homomorphism. Consider the composite of $\mathfrak{X}$'s component morphism `𝔛.compZero κ`, defined on the scheme $(\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{ratModel}\ \kappa).C$ with target the fibre product of `DRModel.toBase p` with $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$, followed by `DRModel.baseChangeMap toκ`, the induced morphism from that fibre product to the fibre product of `DRModel.toBase p` with $\operatorname{Spec} O \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$ (obtained from the identity on `DRModel p` and $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathrm{to}\kappa$). The assertion is that the set-theoretic range of the underlying continuous map of this composite equals the closure of the singleton consisting of the image of the generic point of $(\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{ratModel}\ \kappa).C$, and that this range is a closed subset.
--
--   This identifies the image of the "zero" component of the geometric special fibre inside $\mathfrak{X} \times_{\mathbb{Z}} \operatorname{Spec} O$ as an irreducible closed subset, namely the closure of the image of its generic point. It is used in the local analysis of the Deligne–Rapoport model at $p$, for instance in the statements producing a residue-field point in the image of the base-change map, the description of the maximal ideal at a crossing in terms of the branch ideal and $p$, and the stalk computations at crossing points.
